---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

// One row per trigger — the situation you are already in — not per technology
// and not per company size. Kept in step with blueprints/README.md in the
// repository; if the two disagree, the repository is right.
const triggers = [
  {
    trigger: "You are starting a public repository",
    blueprint: "open-source",
    status: "draft",
    what:
      "Governance, contribution and CI files, with the decisions already made and the reasons stated. No AI files, no application code, and no licence chosen for you — that one is yours, and it is the choice that decides whether this is open source at all.",
  },
  {
    trigger: "An agent is going to work in your repository",
    blueprint: "ai-assisted-development",
    status: "draft",
    what:
      "The frame the agent operates inside: when it stops and asks, what it may never do, and what has to be verified before anything reaches the default branch. Drops on top of a repository you already have.",
  },
  {
    trigger: "A second person has to be able to trust what is written down",
    blueprint: "agent-maintained-knowledge-base",
    status: "planned",
    what:
      "An operating contract the agent reads, two status axes on every knowledge object, and review as a pull request. Because a recommendation can be reviewed, published, and still be a recommendation — and without the second axis, opinion quietly becomes fact.",
  },
  {
    trigger: "More than one AI client needs the same tools",
    blueprint: "agent-tool-access",
    status: "planned",
    what:
      "One tool surface every client can reach, with the credential held by the runtime rather than the model. Blocked on a question nobody has answered: at what point one surface beats four direct integrations.",
  },
];

const repoUrl = "https://github.com/rubennati/ai-project-standard";
---

<Base
  title="Use cases — AI Standard"
  description="What has to have happened to you before a blueprint is worth taking. One per trigger, not one per technology."
  schemaType="CollectionPage"
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Use cases</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl opacity-80">
    Each of these is a <strong>trigger</strong> — something that has already happened to you. You do not adopt
    the next stage because it exists; you add a control when a concrete problem needs one.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl opacity-80">
    That is why the list is not cut by technology or by team size. Cut by technology, you would have to know the
    answer before you could find it. Cut by size, it would be wrong in both directions: one expert can need more
    technique than a company, and a company can need governance while its AI use is close to zero.
  </p>

  <section class="mt-12 flex flex-col gap-6">
    {
      triggers.map((row, i) => (
        <article class="flex flex-col gap-3 rounded-lg border border-black/10 p-6 dark:border-white/10">
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-3">
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">
              {i + 1}. {row.trigger}
            </h2>
            <span class="rounded-full border border-black/15 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide opacity-70 dark:border-white/15">
              {row.status}
            </span>
          </div>
          <p class="opacity-80">{row.what}</p>
          <p class="text-sm opacity-70">
            Blueprint:{" "}
            <a href={`${repoUrl}/tree/main/blueprints/${row.blueprint}`} rel="noopener">
              <code>{row.blueprint}</code>
            </a>
          </p>
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </section>

  <section class="mt-16 flex flex-col gap-3">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">What the statuses mean</h2>
    <p class="opacity-80">
      <strong>Draft</strong> means the payload is complete and has been run against a fresh copy, but has not yet
      started a real project. <strong>Planned</strong> means the scope is decided and nothing is built — no files,
      and the open questions written down where you can read them.
    </p>
    <p class="opacity-80">
      Planned is not a placeholder. Scoping is the harder half: what a blueprint refuses to do is the part that
      stops it drifting into whatever was easy to write. A planned blueprint that cannot say what it leaves out
      has not been thought about.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12 flex flex-col gap-3">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Three triggers with no blueprint of their own</h2>
    <p class="opacity-80">
      Write access, sensitive data, and more than one user. Each one changes what you need — but they are
      controls that belong <em>inside</em> the blueprints above, not beside them. A separate security blueprint
      would model security as an optional extra, which is the exact failure it would exist to prevent.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-16 flex flex-col gap-3">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Next step</h2>
    <p class="opacity-80">
      A blueprint is material you copy, not something you read. The contract — what one contains, the four
      questions each has to answer, and how the set is cut — is in the repository documentation.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
      <a
        href={`${base}docs/blueprints`}
        class="rounded-md bg-[var(--color-accent)] px-5 py-2.5 font-medium text-[var(--color-on-accent)] no-underline hover:opacity-90"
      >
        What a blueprint is
      </a>
      <a
        href={`${base}docs/getting-started`}
        class="rounded-md border border-black/15 px-5 py-2.5 font-medium no-underline hover:bg-black/5 dark:border-white/15 dark:hover:bg-white/5"
      >
        Getting started
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
